# Internal operator dashboard — spec

A separate small app, outside `src/`, on its own hostname (`ops.abcd.com`),
reading the same Postgres. Clean slate: none of the main app's Tailwind /
Radix / Base UI setup comes along.

The backend it needs already exists — this file is what to build against.
See `openwiki/observability-metering.md` for how the data gets there and
`openwiki/deployment-runbook.md` §8 for the manual grants.

Nothing here is user-facing. The user-facing usage view is a separate job, built
later inside `src/` with the existing components.


## 0. The one rule

This app is the least-hardened thing that will ever touch production data, and
it can read every user's workspace names, file names, and chat volume. Design
accordingly:

- **Read-only database role for v1 charts.** Not the gateway's credentials. The
  `evo_ops` role in the runbook grants `SELECT` on eight tables and nothing
  else. If the app is compromised, the blast radius is disclosure, not
  destruction.
- **The one exception is the model-registry grid (§6).** That screen is why
  `operators.role = 'admin'` exists. It needs a *second* DB role with a
  tight write grant (not the gateway role, not `evo_ops`). Viewer sessions
  must not even be issued that credential. Until §6 ships, there are still
  **no mutation endpoints**.
- **No customer content.** Show workspace *names* and file *names* if needed for
  support, never material bodies, chat messages, or note content. If a support
  workflow needs that, it needs consent, not a dashboard.


## 1. Authentication

`operators` already exists:

```sql
CREATE TABLE operators (
  user_id      text PRIMARY KEY REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
  role         text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'viewer' CHECK (role IN ('viewer','admin')),
  note         text NOT NULL DEFAULT '',
  created_at   timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  last_seen_at timestamptz
);
```

`user_id` is the Clerk user id. **Membership is granted only by hand**
(`INSERT` in psql) — there is deliberately no API for it, because an escalation
path reachable from the product turns every product bug into a path to
everyone's data.

`role` gates the model-registry write screen (`admin` only). `viewer` stays
read-only everywhere else. Membership is still granted only by hand.

### Recommended: Cloudflare Access in front, Clerk inside

Two independent gates, either of which alone stops an attacker:

1. **Cloudflare Access** on `ops.abcd.com`, restricted to a named email list or
   your Google Workspace. This runs at the edge — the app is never reachable
   without passing it, so an auth bug in the app is not exploitable from the
   internet. Access injects a signed `Cf-Access-Jwt-Assertion` header; verify it
   against Cloudflare's public keys, do not just trust its presence.
2. **The same Clerk instance** inside the app, then `SELECT 1 FROM operators
   WHERE user_id = $1`. No second identity system to run, and removing someone
   from the product removes their ops access in the same action.

This is worth the extra setup here specifically because the failure mode is
"someone reads every customer's data" rather than "someone reads their own".

### Alternatives considered

| Option | For | Against |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Same Clerk instance + `operators` check** (no Access) | Nothing new to run; one identity; revoking product access revokes ops access | A single bug in the operators check is total exposure, and the app is publicly reachable while you find it |
| **Separate Clerk application** | An ops session can never be minted by the product, so product token bugs cannot reach ops | Second Clerk app to configure and pay for; operators maintain two logins; `operators.user_id` no longer matches `users.id`, so the FK has to go and the join with product data gets manual |
| **Cloudflare Access only**, app trusts the header | Simplest app code — no auth logic at all | The origin must be genuinely unreachable except through Cloudflare, or the header is forgeable and it is a fully open admin panel. Also gives no per-operator identity in the database for `last_seen_at` |
| **Basic auth / shared password** | Trivial | One shared secret, no audit trail, no revocation for one person, and it will end up in a chat message |

Whatever is chosen: update `operators.last_seen_at` on each session so a
long-dormant grant is visible and can be cleaned up.


## 2. Data it reads

All of these already exist and are populated. Prefer `usage_daily` for anything
charted — **dashboard queries must never scan `usage_events`**, which is the
same database serving live chat requests.

| Table | Contains | Use for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `usage_daily` | pre-aggregated by day × user × kind × surface × provider × model | every chart and total |
| `usage_events` | raw ledger, `trace_id` per row | drill-down for one user or one trace only, always with a `LIMIT` |
| `user_credits` | `used_micros`, `reserved_micros`, `period_start` | current-period balances |
| `credit_reservations` | open holds | health: stuck reservations mean settles are failing |
| `user_storage` / `user_storage_deltas` | byte counters | storage totals; effective used = `used_bytes` + sum of deltas |
| `users` | `plan_tier`, lifecycle timestamps | plan mix, account states |
| `workspaces` | names, owner | support lookup |
| `jobs` | ingest queue | backlog depth, failure rate |
| `email_outbox` | mail queue | delivery failures |

Units: `credit_micros` is millionths of a credit; 1 credit ≈ 1000 output tokens
of the standard chat model. Divide by 1,000,000 to display. Free tier is 1,000
credits/month, Pro 20,000 (`server/internal/store/credits.go`).


## 3. Screens

Ordered by how often you will actually open them.

### 3.1 Overview

The page that answers "is anything on fire, and what did today cost".

- Credits consumed today / this month, split by `kind` (llm, embedding,
  caption, parse_gpu, email) — stacked area over 30 days.
- Same split by `surface` (chat, generate, editor, ingest) — this is the one
  that tells you which *product feature* is expensive, which is the number that
  changes decisions.
- Top 20 users by credits this period, with plan tier. Expect one or two users
  to be most of the bill.
- Storage total and top consumers.
- Counters: signups today, active workspaces (7d), ingest jobs queued /
  running / failed (24h).

### 3.2 Health

Things that are silently broken. Each of these is a real failure mode with no
other surface:

- **Open reservations past `expires_at`** — should be near zero. Non-zero means
  the sweeper is not running.
- **`usage_rollup_state.last_run_at`** — stale means the rollup died and every
  chart above is frozen.
- **`jobs` stuck in `running`** beyond a threshold — a worker died mid-job.
- **`email_outbox` failures** in 24h.
- **Chat turns with zero recorded usage** — the signature of a provider path
  that stopped reporting tokens, i.e. spend going unbilled:
  ```sql
  SELECT count(*) FROM messages m
  WHERE m.role='assistant' AND m.status='complete'
    AND m.created_at > now() - interval '24 hours'
    AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM usage_events e WHERE e.trace_id = m.generation_id);
  ```
  (adjust the join to whatever id is actually available — the point is the check)
- **Ratio of `credit_reservations` settled vs released.** A rising release rate
  means requests are failing after reserving.

### 3.3 User detail

Reached by searching email or user id. For support tickets.

- Plan, account state, `period_start`, credits used / reserved / limit.
- Storage used / reserved / limit.
- Usage over time from `usage_daily`, by kind.
- Their workspaces: name, file count, last activity.
- Last 50 `usage_events` with `trace_id`, so a "why was I charged for this" or
  "this failed" ticket can be traced into Sentry and the logs with one copy-paste.

### 3.4 Cost explorer

- Group `usage_daily` by any of day / user / kind / surface / provider / model.
- Compare `credit_micros` (what users were charged) against your actual provider
  invoices, pasted in manually. **This comparison is the whole point of pricing
  being policy rather than derived** — it is how you find out that a model got
  more expensive, or that some path is spending without being metered. There is
  no automated version of this and there should not be.


## 4. Explicitly out of scope for v1

- Editing anything **except** the model-registry grid in §6 (admin only).
  Quota resets, resend-email, impersonation still stay in `psql`.
- Impersonating a user.
- Reading note, chat, or material content.
- Real-time / websocket updates. A 30-second poll is fine; nobody watches this
  screen continuously, and it shares a database with live traffic.
- Alerting. Sentry covers errors; spend alerts belong in the provider consoles
  (Modal, B2, Sentry) where they can act on their own billing.
- A `modelctl` CLI. Deleted. Do not add it back; the grid is the write path.


## 5. Deployment

- Own container in `deploy/docker-compose.yml`, behind `ops.abcd.com`.
- `DATABASE_URL` uses the `evo_ops` read-only role.
- Registry writes (§6) use a separate `DATABASE_URL` / role, only loaded for
  `admin` sessions. Grant `INSERT`/`UPDATE` on `model_configs`,
  `model_registry_state`, plus the remap/notify tables listed in §6. No
  `DELETE` on `model_configs`.
- Cloudflare: proxied, cache bypassed, Access policy applied.
- No public assets, no SEO, `noindex`.
- `SENTRY_DSN` optional — if added, use a fifth Sentry project so ops noise does
  not mix with product errors.


## 6. Model registry grid (replaces `modelctl`) — not built yet

The product gateway has **no HTTP write path** for the registry on purpose.
`server/cmd/modelctl` is gone. Operators edit the mapping here, then press
**Save** once. Until this ships, the seeded `model_configs` rows are the
registry; changing them is `psql`.

### 6.1 What the operator sees

A matrix:

| | chat | generate | editor | ingest | embedding | vision |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `deepseek-flash` | `(deepseek-flash, v1)` | … | … | … | empty | empty |
| `deepseek-pro` | `(deepseek-pro, v1)` | … | empty | empty | empty | empty |
| `qwen-embed` | empty | empty | empty | empty | `(qwen-embed, v1)` | empty |
| … | | | | | | |

- **Row** = user-facing key. This is the slug in Settings and in
  `users.chat_model_key` / `generate_model_key`. Operator-only keys
  (embedding / vision) still get a row; they just never appear in
  Settings.
- **Column** = surface. Same enum as `model_configs.surfaces`.
- **Cell** = an llm config pin `{model_key, version}`, or empty. Empty means
  this user-facing key does not serve that surface. The pin's `model_key`
  is usually the row key; allowing a different key is an alias (row slug
  stays stable, config behind it can move). Decide at implement time
  whether aliases are allowed — if not, the cell key is always the row key
  and the operator only picks a version / a catalog row of that key.
- One cell per column is the **surface default** (`is_default_for`). Mark
  it on the grid. New accounts snapshot that key. Editor / ingest /
  embedding / vision are not user-selectable; their default cell is
  the live default the gateway/pipeline poll.

Clicking a filled cell opens the config: `display_name`, `provider_slug`,
`base_url`, `provider_model_id`, `params`, credit micros, USD micros,
`enabled`, `surfaces`, created_at. On embedding rows `enabled`,
`provider_model_id`, and `params` are read-only. `base_url` and
`provider_slug` stay editable so the same weights can move host. Show
the provider credential as a **masked env lookup** (`provider_slug` →
process env). API keys are not stored on `model_configs` and must not be
pasted into the dashboard.

From that drawer the operator can **change** the cell (pick another catalog
config, or add a new immutable version) or **clear** it (this key no longer
serves this surface). Edits stay local until Save.

A catalog of all `model_configs` rows (including disabled) sits behind the
grid so a pin can still be inspected after it was retired. Disabled rows
are how in-flight assistant / ingest pins keep resolving — **never DELETE**.

### 6.2 Today's schema (the grid is a pivot)

There is no assignment table. Each `model_configs` row is immutable
`(model_key, version)` with a `surfaces[]` and optional `is_default_for[]`.
User prefs store a **key only**; resolve is "latest enabled version of that
key that lists this surface".

So the live grid is derived:

```
for each distinct model_key:
  for each surface:
    cell = latest enabled row of that key with surface ∈ surfaces
           else empty
```

Save compiles the dirty grid back into row operations, in **one
transaction**, then `UPDATE model_registry_state SET version = version + 1`.
Do not mutate an existing version in place (rates, url, provider id,
surfaces). That would reprice pins already stamped on messages/jobs.
Instead: `INSERT` a new version, disable versions no remaining cell
points at. **Embedding is the exception:** insert the new row and leave
every older embedding row enabled and pointed at the same
`provider_model_id` / `params`. Postgres refuses disable, delete,
stripping or adding the embedding surface, and in-place identity
rewrites (`protect_embedding_model_configs`). Same width is a different
space and a different table. A later 2560-d model is a new catalog row
plus a new `rag_chunk_vectors_*` table, not an UPDATE of `qwen-embed`
and not a write into `rag_chunk_vectors_2560`.

Vision is a normal hot-reloadable column: retargeting it applies to jobs
enqueued after the poll picks it up, and queued jobs keep their pin.

**Embedding needs an explicit acknowledgement step in the UI**, because
retargeting it is neither a hot reload nor a migration. Every workspace pins
an embedding model at creation and keeps it for life
(`workspaces.embedding_model_key`), so a new default reaches only workspaces
created afterwards, and there is no reindex job. Save must make the operator
confirm, in these words or close to them:

- existing workspaces will keep using their current embedding model, forever;
- that model's row stays enabled and its provider must stay reachable,
  because those workspaces resolve it on every search and upload;
- retrieval quality now differs by workspace creation date.

The embedding column has no disable, no clear, no deprecate, and no
in-place model swap. The drawer must not offer `enabled = false` or a
`provider_model_id` / `params` edit on a row whose `surfaces` include
`embedding`. Adding a default is "insert a new catalog row, give it its own vector
table, clear the old `is_default_for`, mark the new row default"; the
previous default stays in the catalog, locked on. Two rows cannot list
the same surface in `is_default_for`.

Two hard refusals belong here, not just a warning:

- **Never disable, delete, strip, or rewrite an embedding row.** Not even
  when another model of the same width exists. The Save transaction will
  hit the same trigger as `psql`. Do not catch-and-retry by rewriting
  `provider_model_id`.
- **A new embedding model is a schema change, not a Save.** Even at the
  same width. An embedding row whose `params.vector_table` is missing, or
  whose pin is not in the Go/Python allowlists, must not become the
  default. The UI should say so rather than surfacing a constraint
  violation.

Worth showing on this screen: per-dimension and per-model workspace counts
(`GROUP BY embedding_dim, embedding_model_key`), so you can see which pins
still have live workspaces without writing SQL. Every embedding key in
that list is permanent.

### 6.3 What Save must refuse

Same invariants `modelctl` used to enforce, now checked against the whole
draft so a batch cannot leave the registry empty:

- Every surface that currently has a default still has a default cell after
  Save. Retargeting a chat/generate/editor/ingest/vision default is
  "clear this cell + mark another", not "leave the column with no
  default". Embedding retarget is only "mark another"; the previous
  default row stays enabled.
- Clearing the last chat/generate/editor cell of a user-facing key is
  **deprecate**, not a quiet empty. Operator must name a fallback key that
  still has that surface in the *saved* grid. Then, in the same
  transaction: remap `users.chat_model_key` / `generate_model_key` /
  `editor_model_key`, disable every enabled version of the retired key,
  notify (below). Editor is a user preference too as of the editor picker in
  Settings → LLM; a remap that forgets it leaves those users failing with
  `model_unavailable` on every keystroke that triggers the copilot.
  Embedding keys are not in this flow. There is no user pref to remap, and
  the row cannot be disabled.
- A cell cannot point at a missing / disabled catalog row.
- Do not disable a version that is still the default for a surface; move
  the default first (or as an earlier step in the same batch).
- Do not disable, delete, clear, or rewrite `provider_model_id` /
  `params` on an embedding cell, and do not run deprecate against a key
  that serves `embedding`.
- Two admins saving at once: lock `model_configs` (and remapped users)
  in the transaction; bump is the cache invalidation.

Draft validation in the UI should catch most of this before the request
leaves the browser. The transaction is the source of truth.

### 6.4 Notifications already exist — reuse them

Do not invent a second mail path. Deprecate already has:

- in-app `kind: system`, `data.code = model_deprecated`,
  `fromKey` / `fromName` / `toKey` / `toName`, `href: /settings?tab=llm`
- email template `model-deprecated`, category `billing` (muteable),
  idempotency `model-deprecated:{fromKey}:{toKey}:{userId}`
- copy in `messages/*.json`, `emails/model-deprecated.tsx`, dispatcher
  in `server/cmd/api/email.go`

Call `store.NotifyTx` from the Save transaction for remapped users.
Sequential notify is fine at current user counts.

There is still **no request-time fallback**. A pref that names a key with
no enabled chat/generate row fails `model_unavailable`. Save must remap
before disable so that window does not exist.

### 6.5 Product API stays read-only

`GET /api/models` and `PATCH /api/me/models` are the user picker. Do not
add registry writes to the gateway. Ops is a separate app, admin-only,
own write role. A compromised session on `app.abcd.com` must not be able
to retarget everyone's default model.

### 6.6 Implementation notes (when building)

- Gate the screen on `operators.role = 'admin'`. Viewers can see the
  matrix read-only (from `evo_ops` SELECT, already granted on
  `model_configs` / `model_registry_state`).
- Local dirty state; Save is one POST of the full grid (or a list of cell
  ops). Partial autosave will race and skip deprecate.
- Adding a **new row** (new user-facing key) is "add catalog config + fill
  cells", not a separate CLI.
- `server/internal/models` and `pipeline/pipeline/registry.py` already
  poll `model_registry_state` every 30s. Save only needs to bump.
- Tests belong next to the ops write handler, not a resurrected CLI.
  Cover: version bump (disable old, insert new, prefs unchanged) for
  chat/generate/editor only, last chat cell of a key requires fallback +
  remap + mail, refuse Save that drops a surface default, never DELETE
  `model_configs`, refuse disable/delete/strip/rewrite of any embedding
  row, and allow inserting a second same-width embedding row as a new
  key.
